1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
Sweetheart, you're nine now,

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:02,786
aren't you?

3
00:00:02,966 --> 00:00:03,848
Nine.

4
00:00:04,028 --> 00:00:05,491
That's a good age.

5
00:00:05,671 --> 00:00:07,761
I remember nine very well.

6
00:00:07,941 --> 00:00:10,548
It was nineteen fifty-three,

7
00:00:10,548 --> 00:00:12,318
in Mansfield, Ohio.

8
00:00:12,498 --> 00:00:13,714
A new president,

9
00:00:13,714 --> 00:00:14,626
a new queen,

10
00:00:14,626 --> 00:00:16,678
a war that finally stopped,

11
00:00:16,678 --> 00:00:19,338
and the first television set on our

12
00:00:19,338 --> 00:00:19,870
street.

13
00:00:20,050 --> 00:00:21,915
And I had a pair of roller

14
00:00:21,915 --> 00:00:23,996
skates with a key that I wore

15
00:00:23,996 --> 00:00:25,933
on a string around my neck,

16
00:00:25,933 --> 00:00:26,865
like a medal.

17
00:00:27,216 --> 00:00:29,335
Let me take you through the whole

18
00:00:29,335 --> 00:00:30,106
year, honey.

19
00:00:30,286 --> 00:00:31,273
What we ate,

20
00:00:31,273 --> 00:00:32,589
what we watched,

21
00:00:32,589 --> 00:00:33,987
what things cost,

22
00:00:33,987 --> 00:00:36,207
and what we were afraid of.

23
00:00:36,387 --> 00:00:39,225
Because nineteen fifty-three was the year a

24
00:00:39,225 --> 00:00:41,270
lot of the America you know got

25
00:00:41,270 --> 00:00:41,798
started.

26
00:00:41,978 --> 00:00:44,427
And some of it is gone forever.

27
00:00:44,778 --> 00:00:46,969
In January, on the twentieth,

28
00:00:46,969 --> 00:00:49,689
we watched a new president sworn in.

29
00:00:49,869 --> 00:00:52,799
General Eisenhower, the man who won the

30
00:00:52,799 --> 00:00:53,851
war in Europe.

31
00:00:54,031 --> 00:00:54,843
Ike.

32
00:00:55,023 --> 00:00:57,378
My father had served under him,

33
00:00:57,378 --> 00:00:58,062
in a way,

34
00:00:58,062 --> 00:01:00,720
along with a few million other men,

35
00:01:00,720 --> 00:01:02,847
and he stood up in our front

36
00:01:02,847 --> 00:01:05,050
room when Ike put his hand on

37
00:01:05,050 --> 00:01:05,809
the Bible.

38
00:01:05,989 --> 00:01:08,503
My father never stood up for anything

39
00:01:08,503 --> 00:01:10,610
on the radio or the television.

40
00:01:10,790 --> 00:01:12,555
He stood up for Ike.

41
00:01:12,904 --> 00:01:15,286
We watched it at the Kowalskis' house,

42
00:01:15,286 --> 00:01:17,856
because they had the first television set

43
00:01:17,856 --> 00:01:18,671
on the block,

44
00:01:18,671 --> 00:01:21,304
a big wooden Admiral with a round-cornered

45
00:01:21,304 --> 00:01:23,435
screen the size of a dinner plate.

46
00:01:23,615 --> 00:01:26,344
The picture was gray and snowy and

47
00:01:26,344 --> 00:01:28,591
rolled up every few minutes,

48
00:01:28,591 --> 00:01:29,153
and Mr.

49
00:01:29,333 --> 00:01:31,885
Kowalski would get up and bang the

50
00:01:31,885 --> 00:01:32,711
side of it.

51
00:01:32,891 --> 00:01:35,789
Fifteen people sat on that living room

52
00:01:35,789 --> 00:01:36,246
floor.

53
00:01:36,597 --> 00:01:39,714
The very night before the inauguration,

54
00:01:39,714 --> 00:01:40,673
on a Monday,

55
00:01:40,673 --> 00:01:42,111
Lucy had her baby.

56
00:01:42,291 --> 00:01:43,700
On I Love Lucy,

57
00:01:43,700 --> 00:01:44,358
I mean.

58
00:01:44,538 --> 00:01:47,010
Little Ricky was born on the television

59
00:01:47,010 --> 00:01:49,229
the same night Lucille Ball had her

60
00:01:49,229 --> 00:01:50,877
real baby in the hospital.

61
00:01:51,057 --> 00:01:53,714
Forty-four million people watched it,

62
00:01:53,714 --> 00:01:54,145
honey.

63
00:01:54,325 --> 00:01:56,125
More than watched Ike.

64
00:01:56,305 --> 00:01:59,059
The whole country was talking about a

65
00:01:59,059 --> 00:02:00,995
baby on a television show.

66
00:02:01,345 --> 00:02:03,377
And you know what's funny?

67
00:02:03,557 --> 00:02:04,403
On that show,

68
00:02:04,403 --> 00:02:06,745
they weren't even allowed to say the

69
00:02:06,745 --> 00:02:07,656
word pregnant.

70
00:02:07,836 --> 00:02:10,111
The network wouldn't let them.

71
00:02:10,291 --> 00:02:12,253
They had to say expecting.

72
00:02:12,433 --> 00:02:15,421
Lucy and Ricky slept in two separate

73
00:02:15,421 --> 00:02:16,831
little beds, too,

74
00:02:16,831 --> 00:02:19,901
even though they were married in real

75
00:02:19,901 --> 00:02:20,316
life.

76
00:02:20,496 --> 00:02:23,840
That's how television was in nineteen fifty-three.

77
00:02:24,189 --> 00:02:26,293
I was in fourth grade at Bowman

78
00:02:26,293 --> 00:02:27,243
Street School.

79
00:02:27,423 --> 00:02:29,558
We sat in rows of wooden desks

80
00:02:29,558 --> 00:02:30,981
bolted to the floor,

81
00:02:30,981 --> 00:02:33,045
with a hole in the corner for

82
00:02:33,045 --> 00:02:36,034
an inkwell, and we learned penmanship with

83
00:02:36,034 --> 00:02:37,315
the Palmer Method.

84
00:02:37,495 --> 00:02:39,330
Ovals and push-pulls,

85
00:02:39,330 --> 00:02:42,127
all day, until your wrist ached.

86
00:02:42,307 --> 00:02:43,831
If you were left-handed,

87
00:02:43,831 --> 00:02:46,117
the teacher made you write with your

88
00:02:46,117 --> 00:02:46,498
right.

89
00:02:46,678 --> 00:02:49,081
My friend Linda had her left hand

90
00:02:49,081 --> 00:02:50,684
tied behind her chair.

91
00:02:50,864 --> 00:02:52,106
Can you imagine?

92
00:02:52,455 --> 00:02:54,750
Every morning we said the Pledge of

93
00:02:54,750 --> 00:02:56,914
Allegiance and the Lord's Prayer.

94
00:02:57,094 --> 00:02:59,964
Then milk, in little glass bottles,

95
00:02:59,964 --> 00:03:01,194
two cents each,

96
00:03:01,194 --> 00:03:03,326
warm by the time it got to

97
00:03:03,326 --> 00:03:03,572
us.

98
00:03:03,752 --> 00:03:06,992
The vaccine for polio wasn't ready yet,

99
00:03:06,992 --> 00:03:07,490
honey.

100
00:03:07,670 --> 00:03:09,679
That came two years later.

101
00:03:09,859 --> 00:03:12,965
In fifty-three we were still just afraid,

102
00:03:12,965 --> 00:03:14,480
every single summer.

103
00:03:14,829 --> 00:03:17,460
At recess the girls played jacks on

104
00:03:17,460 --> 00:03:19,640
the sidewalk and jumped rope,

105
00:03:19,640 --> 00:03:22,121
double Dutch, singing the rhymes.

106
00:03:22,301 --> 00:03:24,600
Cinderella, dressed in yella,

107
00:03:24,600 --> 00:03:26,979
went upstairs to kiss a fella.

108
00:03:27,159 --> 00:03:29,600
The boys played marbles for keeps,

109
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:32,040
and if you lost your best shooter,

110
00:03:32,040 --> 00:03:33,475
you lost it forever.

111
00:03:33,655 --> 00:03:36,284
Danny lost his whole bag of cat's-eyes

112
00:03:36,284 --> 00:03:38,637
to a fifth grader in one afternoon

113
00:03:38,637 --> 00:03:40,505
and cried all the way home.

114
00:03:40,855 --> 00:03:43,805
After school, it was hopscotch drawn with

115
00:03:43,805 --> 00:03:45,028
a piece of chalk,

116
00:03:45,028 --> 00:03:48,193
and kick-the-can in the street until supper.

117
00:03:48,373 --> 00:03:51,126
There were maybe twenty children on Elm

118
00:03:51,126 --> 00:03:53,597
Street, and we all played together,

119
00:03:53,597 --> 00:03:55,362
big ones and little ones.

120
00:03:55,542 --> 00:03:57,319
No one organized it.

121
00:03:57,499 --> 00:03:59,971
No one drove us anywhere.

122
00:04:00,151 --> 00:04:02,399
You just walked out your front door

123
00:04:02,399 --> 00:04:04,389
and the game was already going.

124
00:04:04,739 --> 00:04:07,340
In June, a young woman was crowned

125
00:04:07,340 --> 00:04:08,640
queen in England.

126
00:04:08,820 --> 00:04:10,376
Queen Elizabeth.

127
00:04:10,556 --> 00:04:12,541
She was only twenty-seven.

128
00:04:12,721 --> 00:04:15,189
They flew the film across the ocean

129
00:04:15,189 --> 00:04:17,728
on airplanes, and it was on American

130
00:04:17,728 --> 00:04:19,420
television the same day.

131
00:04:19,600 --> 00:04:21,063
My mother cried.

132
00:04:21,243 --> 00:04:23,945
She'd never been to England and never

133
00:04:23,945 --> 00:04:25,989
would, but she cried anyway,

134
00:04:25,989 --> 00:04:28,399
watching that girl in that crown,

135
00:04:28,399 --> 00:04:31,100
because she said she looked so alone.

136
00:04:31,450 --> 00:04:32,942
For weeks after that,

137
00:04:32,942 --> 00:04:35,642
every girl on Elm Street played queen.

138
00:04:35,822 --> 00:04:38,213
We made crowns out of the gold

139
00:04:38,213 --> 00:04:41,003
foil from Hershey bars and wore our

140
00:04:41,003 --> 00:04:43,554
mothers' old curtains for robes.

141
00:04:43,734 --> 00:04:46,021
Linda was always the queen.

142
00:04:46,201 --> 00:04:48,895
I was always the lady-in-waiting.

143
00:04:49,075 --> 00:04:50,352
I didn't mind.

144
00:04:50,532 --> 00:04:52,621
Well, I minded a little.

145
00:04:52,971 --> 00:04:55,735
Our street had visitors every week.

146
00:04:55,915 --> 00:04:58,523
The milkman, early in the morning,

147
00:04:58,523 --> 00:05:00,593
rattling the glass bottles.

148
00:05:00,773 --> 00:05:03,037
The bread man from the bakery.

149
00:05:03,217 --> 00:05:05,851
The Fuller Brush man with his suitcase

150
00:05:05,851 --> 00:05:08,415
of brushes, who always gave my mother

151
00:05:08,415 --> 00:05:11,049
a free little vegetable brush just for

152
00:05:11,049 --> 00:05:12,227
opening the door.

153
00:05:12,407 --> 00:05:14,004
And the knife sharpener,

154
00:05:14,004 --> 00:05:16,267
who rang a bell and sharpened your

155
00:05:16,267 --> 00:05:18,530
scissors on a stone wheel right on

156
00:05:18,530 --> 00:05:20,127
the sidewalk for a dime.

157
00:05:20,477 --> 00:05:22,755
On Sundays we went to church at

158
00:05:22,755 --> 00:05:25,179
Trinity Lutheran, all dressed up.

159
00:05:25,359 --> 00:05:27,382
I had white gloves and a hat

160
00:05:27,382 --> 00:05:29,405
with elastic under the chin,

161
00:05:29,405 --> 00:05:31,644
and Danny had a clip-on bow tie

162
00:05:31,644 --> 00:05:33,884
he pulled off the minute we got

163
00:05:33,884 --> 00:05:34,462
outside.

164
00:05:34,642 --> 00:05:37,556
Then Sunday dinner at one o'clock.

165
00:05:37,736 --> 00:05:40,273
Pot roast, mashed potatoes,

166
00:05:40,273 --> 00:05:42,904
green beans from the garden,

167
00:05:42,904 --> 00:05:45,723
and Grandma Pearl's apple pie.

168
00:05:45,903 --> 00:05:48,109
Every Sunday, the same.

169
00:05:48,289 --> 00:05:50,673
I could cook it with my eyes

170
00:05:50,673 --> 00:05:51,610
closed now.

171
00:05:51,959 --> 00:05:54,351
After dinner my father took us for

172
00:05:54,351 --> 00:05:56,039
a drive in the Plymouth,

173
00:05:56,039 --> 00:05:57,375
out past the farms,

174
00:05:57,375 --> 00:05:59,696
and we stopped at the Dairy Queen

175
00:05:59,696 --> 00:06:00,470
for a cone.

176
00:06:00,650 --> 00:06:02,449
A nickel for a small one.

177
00:06:02,629 --> 00:06:04,520
My mother had hers in a cup

178
00:06:04,520 --> 00:06:06,832
so it wouldn't drip on her dress.

179
00:06:07,012 --> 00:06:09,471
My father always ate his in about

180
00:06:09,471 --> 00:06:12,154
three bites and then looked sadly at

181
00:06:12,154 --> 00:06:13,421
the empty napkin.

182
00:06:13,601 --> 00:06:15,111
That was Sunday,

183
00:06:15,111 --> 00:06:17,188
every week, for years.

184
00:06:17,538 --> 00:06:20,545
Summer in nineteen fifty-three was hot.

185
00:06:20,725 --> 00:06:22,328
No air conditioning,

186
00:06:22,328 --> 00:06:23,129
of course.

187
00:06:23,309 --> 00:06:25,666
We slept with the windows open and

188
00:06:25,666 --> 00:06:27,955
a wet washcloth on our foreheads,

189
00:06:27,955 --> 00:06:30,590
and on the worst nights Father dragged

190
00:06:30,590 --> 00:06:33,572
the mattresses out onto the screened porch.

191
00:06:33,752 --> 00:06:36,239
You could hear every cricket and every

192
00:06:36,239 --> 00:06:37,548
car on Route Thirty.

193
00:06:37,728 --> 00:06:38,773
I loved it.

194
00:06:39,123 --> 00:06:41,503
In the daytime we disappeared.

195
00:06:41,683 --> 00:06:44,549
Danny and I would leave after breakfast

196
00:06:44,549 --> 00:06:47,489
and come home when the streetlights came

197
00:06:47,489 --> 00:06:47,709
on.

198
00:06:47,889 --> 00:06:50,670
We rode our bicycles everywhere,

199
00:06:50,670 --> 00:06:53,973
no helmets, sometimes two on one bike.

200
00:06:54,153 --> 00:06:56,321
We went to the creek and caught

201
00:06:56,321 --> 00:06:56,951
crayfish.

202
00:06:57,131 --> 00:06:59,615
We drank out of the garden hose.

203
00:06:59,795 --> 00:07:02,042
Nobody knew where we were,

204
00:07:02,042 --> 00:07:03,685
and nobody worried.

205
00:07:04,034 --> 00:07:06,968
And on Saturday afternoons there was the

206
00:07:06,968 --> 00:07:08,655
movie matinee downtown.

207
00:07:08,835 --> 00:07:10,526
A quarter got you in,

208
00:07:10,526 --> 00:07:12,539
a nickel got you popcorn.

209
00:07:12,719 --> 00:07:14,452
A newsreel, a cartoon,

210
00:07:14,452 --> 00:07:17,053
a serial that ended with the hero

211
00:07:17,053 --> 00:07:18,629
hanging off a cliff,

212
00:07:18,629 --> 00:07:20,126
and then the movie.

213
00:07:20,306 --> 00:07:22,553
That summer it was Peter Pan,

214
00:07:22,553 --> 00:07:23,870
from Walt Disney.

215
00:07:24,050 --> 00:07:25,664
I went three times.

216
00:07:25,844 --> 00:07:27,854
Danny went to see a movie in

217
00:07:27,854 --> 00:07:29,864
three-D, with paper glasses,

218
00:07:29,864 --> 00:07:32,664
and screamed when the creature came out

219
00:07:32,664 --> 00:07:33,669
of the screen.

220
00:07:34,019 --> 00:07:36,281
On the twenty-seventh of July,

221
00:07:36,281 --> 00:07:38,995
the radio said the fighting in Korea

222
00:07:38,995 --> 00:07:39,674
was over.

223
00:07:39,854 --> 00:07:41,978
An armistice, they called it.

224
00:07:42,158 --> 00:07:43,465
Not a victory,

225
00:07:43,465 --> 00:07:44,585
just a stop.

226
00:07:44,765 --> 00:07:46,576
Three years of fighting,

227
00:07:46,576 --> 00:07:50,123
and more than thirty-six thousand American boys

228
00:07:50,123 --> 00:07:50,500
gone.

229
00:07:50,680 --> 00:07:52,876
Two of them from Mansfield that I

230
00:07:52,876 --> 00:07:53,408
knew of.

231
00:07:53,588 --> 00:07:55,596
The older brother of a boy in

232
00:07:55,596 --> 00:07:57,327
my class never came home.

233
00:07:57,676 --> 00:07:59,546
There were no parades,

234
00:07:59,546 --> 00:08:00,057
honey.

235
00:08:00,237 --> 00:08:02,454
Not like nineteen forty-five.

236
00:08:02,634 --> 00:08:04,294
People were just tired.

237
00:08:04,474 --> 00:08:06,252
My father read the paper,

238
00:08:06,252 --> 00:08:08,385
folded it, and went out to mow

239
00:08:08,385 --> 00:08:09,025
the lawn.

240
00:08:09,205 --> 00:08:11,198
He'd been called up for Korea in

241
00:08:11,198 --> 00:08:13,689
nineteen fifty and then they didn't need

242
00:08:13,689 --> 00:08:14,561
him after all,

243
00:08:14,561 --> 00:08:16,741
and I think he felt something about

244
00:08:16,741 --> 00:08:17,924
that he never said.

245
00:08:18,104 --> 00:08:20,740
He mowed that lawn for two hours.

246
00:08:21,089 --> 00:08:22,866
Now, prices.

247
00:08:23,046 --> 00:08:25,865
A loaf of bread was about sixteen

248
00:08:25,865 --> 00:08:26,378
cents.

249
00:08:26,558 --> 00:08:27,770
A gallon of milk,

250
00:08:27,770 --> 00:08:29,125
about ninety cents,

251
00:08:29,125 --> 00:08:31,693
delivered to the door by the milkman

252
00:08:31,693 --> 00:08:33,048
in his white truck.

253
00:08:33,228 --> 00:08:35,411
A stamp was three cents.

254
00:08:35,591 --> 00:08:38,563
Gas was about twenty-nine cents a gallon.

255
00:08:38,743 --> 00:08:42,355
A brand-new Chevrolet was around sixteen hundred

256
00:08:42,355 --> 00:08:42,957
dollars.

257
00:08:43,137 --> 00:08:46,051
And my father made about seventy-five dollars

258
00:08:46,051 --> 00:08:48,187
a week at the Westinghouse plant.

259
00:08:48,537 --> 00:08:50,820
And here's a thing that came out

260
00:08:50,820 --> 00:08:51,533
that year.

261
00:08:51,713 --> 00:08:53,025
The TV dinner.

262
00:08:53,205 --> 00:08:56,213
Swanson's, in a little aluminum tray with

263
00:08:56,213 --> 00:08:57,094
three parts.

264
00:08:57,274 --> 00:08:59,461
Turkey, cornbread stuffing,

265
00:08:59,461 --> 00:09:01,404
peas and sweet potatoes,

266
00:09:01,404 --> 00:09:03,752
for about ninety-eight cents.

267
00:09:03,932 --> 00:09:06,313
My mother thought it was a crime.

268
00:09:06,493 --> 00:09:09,472
Grandma Pearl said it was the end

269
00:09:09,472 --> 00:09:10,916
of civilization.

270
00:09:11,096 --> 00:09:13,662
Danny begged for one for a year.

271
00:09:14,011 --> 00:09:16,927
And the new Chevrolet Corvette came out

272
00:09:16,927 --> 00:09:19,543
too, a little white sports car with

273
00:09:19,543 --> 00:09:20,142
no roof.

274
00:09:20,322 --> 00:09:23,111
Only about three hundred were made that

275
00:09:23,111 --> 00:09:23,897
first year.

276
00:09:24,077 --> 00:09:26,273
Not one of them ever drove down

277
00:09:26,273 --> 00:09:28,963
Elm Street, but every boy in Mansfield

278
00:09:28,963 --> 00:09:30,946
had a picture of it torn out

279
00:09:30,946 --> 00:09:31,937
of a magazine.

280
00:09:32,117 --> 00:09:34,368
Danny had his taped over his bed

281
00:09:34,368 --> 00:09:35,775
until he was twelve.

282
00:09:36,124 --> 00:09:38,491
That fall, my mother let me walk

283
00:09:38,491 --> 00:09:41,301
downtown by myself for the first time,

284
00:09:41,301 --> 00:09:42,411
nine years old,

285
00:09:42,411 --> 00:09:44,703
with a dollar in my coat pocket

286
00:09:44,703 --> 00:09:45,517
and a list.

287
00:09:45,697 --> 00:09:47,569
A spool of white thread,

288
00:09:47,569 --> 00:09:48,973
a card of buttons,

289
00:09:48,973 --> 00:09:50,689
and a pound of coffee.

290
00:09:50,869 --> 00:09:53,049
I walked those six blocks like I

291
00:09:53,049 --> 00:09:54,480
owned the whole city.

292
00:09:54,660 --> 00:09:56,718
The lady at the dry goods store

293
00:09:56,718 --> 00:09:58,178
called me Miss Miller.

294
00:09:58,358 --> 00:10:00,587
I came home ten feet tall.

295
00:10:00,937 --> 00:10:02,648
And we were afraid,

296
00:10:02,648 --> 00:10:03,189
honey.

297
00:10:03,369 --> 00:10:05,691
Of different things than you.

298
00:10:05,871 --> 00:10:08,437
Of polio, every summer.

299
00:10:08,617 --> 00:10:10,486
Of the atomic bomb.

300
00:10:10,666 --> 00:10:13,538
The Russians had tested a hydrogen bomb

301
00:10:13,538 --> 00:10:16,410
that August, and in school we practiced

302
00:10:16,410 --> 00:10:18,178
getting under our desks.

303
00:10:18,358 --> 00:10:19,797
Of communists.

304
00:10:19,977 --> 00:10:22,678
Senator McCarthy was on the radio every

305
00:10:22,678 --> 00:10:25,863
week, saying there were communists everywhere.

306
00:10:26,043 --> 00:10:27,216
In the government.

307
00:10:27,396 --> 00:10:28,603
In Hollywood.

308
00:10:28,783 --> 00:10:30,270
Maybe next door.

309
00:10:30,619 --> 00:10:31,502
Mrs.

310
00:10:31,682 --> 00:10:34,042
Pruitt next door said the new family

311
00:10:34,042 --> 00:10:36,009
at the end of the street might

312
00:10:36,009 --> 00:10:38,435
be communists, because they had a lot

313
00:10:38,435 --> 00:10:40,598
of books and didn't go to church.

314
00:10:40,778 --> 00:10:41,966
My mother said,

315
00:10:41,966 --> 00:10:43,867
Edna, they're Lutherans.

316
00:10:44,047 --> 00:10:46,229
They go to the early service.

317
00:10:46,409 --> 00:10:48,406
That was the end of that.

318
00:10:48,586 --> 00:10:50,316
But that's how it was.

319
00:10:50,496 --> 00:10:52,888
Everybody watched everybody.

320
00:10:53,238 --> 00:10:54,870
And then it was December,

321
00:10:54,870 --> 00:10:57,613
and the Kowalskis invited the whole street

322
00:10:57,613 --> 00:10:59,964
over to watch the Christmas shows on

323
00:10:59,964 --> 00:11:01,074
their television.

324
00:11:01,254 --> 00:11:04,246
That winter they even talked about color

325
00:11:04,246 --> 00:11:05,892
television, brand new,

326
00:11:05,892 --> 00:11:08,285
but nobody we knew would see one

327
00:11:08,285 --> 00:11:09,033
for years.

328
00:11:09,213 --> 00:11:11,317
We sat on their floor in our

329
00:11:11,317 --> 00:11:13,420
pajamas, in black and white,

330
00:11:13,420 --> 00:11:16,050
and it was the most wonderful thing

331
00:11:16,050 --> 00:11:17,026
in the world.

332
00:11:17,377 --> 00:11:20,106
On Christmas morning there was a big

333
00:11:20,106 --> 00:11:21,547
box under our tree,

334
00:11:21,547 --> 00:11:23,367
and it was a television.

335
00:11:23,547 --> 00:11:24,650
Our own.

336
00:11:24,830 --> 00:11:27,492
My father had been saving for two

337
00:11:27,492 --> 00:11:27,977
years.

338
00:11:28,157 --> 00:11:30,397
A twenty-one-inch Motorola.

339
00:11:30,577 --> 00:11:31,729
My mother cried,

340
00:11:31,729 --> 00:11:34,033
and Danny ran around the room in

341
00:11:34,033 --> 00:11:36,841
circles, and my father just stood there

342
00:11:36,841 --> 00:11:38,785
with his coffee and smiled.

343
00:11:38,965 --> 00:11:41,242
That was nineteen fifty-three,

344
00:11:41,242 --> 00:11:42,077
sweetheart.

345
00:11:42,257 --> 00:11:44,613
The year the world came into our

346
00:11:44,613 --> 00:11:45,496
living room.

347
00:11:45,847 --> 00:11:47,158
So that was nine.

348
00:11:47,338 --> 00:11:49,414
No phone, no computer,

349
00:11:49,414 --> 00:11:52,621
one television for fifteen people.

350
00:11:52,801 --> 00:11:55,534
We were afraid of things we couldn't

351
00:11:55,534 --> 00:11:58,040
see, and we spent all day outside

352
00:11:58,040 --> 00:11:58,571
anyway.

353
00:11:58,751 --> 00:12:00,758
I don't want you to think it

354
00:12:00,758 --> 00:12:02,478
was perfect, sweetheart.

355
00:12:02,658 --> 00:12:04,481
But I'd give a lot for one

356
00:12:04,481 --> 00:12:06,726
more summer night on that porch,

357
00:12:06,726 --> 00:12:07,708
on a mattress,

358
00:12:07,708 --> 00:12:09,531
listening to the crickets.

359
00:12:09,881 --> 00:12:11,738
Tell me in the comments.

360
00:12:11,918 --> 00:12:13,660
What year were you nine?

361
00:12:13,840 --> 00:12:16,300
And what was the first television in

362
00:12:16,300 --> 00:12:17,462
your family like?

363
00:12:17,642 --> 00:12:19,627
Goodnight, sweetheart.

364
00:12:19,807 --> 00:12:22,549
Grandma Ruth will be right here with

365
00:12:22,549 --> 00:12:24,300
another story tomorrow.
